@node Differential Dataflow
@chapter Differential Dataflow

Differential dataflow is a layer on top of timely dataflow for
performing computations over large collections and @emph{maintaining}
them as the collections change.  A program is written in two easy steps:
write a program against apparently static collections (using idioms that
look like SQL or MapReduce), and then change the inputs.  Differential
dataflow promptly shows the matching changes in the outputs, often in
milliseconds.

As in the timely chapter, every code example here is a complete C
program that compiles against the installed headers; where a book
passage relies on something libdtfl exposes differently, the C way is
described plainly.

